WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
The first film carried epinephrine's signal to PKA.

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.040
Now picture cholera toxin in a gut cell.

3
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.600
It switches nothing ON.

4
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.200
It breaks one OFF reaction.

5
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.180
So why can that kill within hours?

6
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:20.693
Take one switch, Gs alpha.

7
00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:25.553
The receptor pushes it ON, swapping GDP for GTP.

8
00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:30.213
Its own GTPase pulls it OFF, a built-in timer.

9
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:33.973
How much is ON is a contest between them.

10
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:39.687
While Gs alpha holds GTP, its cyclase makes cyclic AMP.

11
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:45.607
In round numbers, 10 a second for about 10 seconds makes about 100.

12
00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:50.607
So an enzyme's gain is its speed, times how long it stays ON.

13
00:00:51.947 --> 00:00:54.627
Slow the timer, and the same cyclase makes more.

14
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.070
Each switch down the row has its own ON and OFF arms.

15
00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.550
And the gains multiply.

16
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:08.190
In teaching numbers, one epinephrine molecule ends as tens of millions of glucose

17
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.090
units. Real gains differ between cell types.

18
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:18.303
The one idea: every switch is pushed ON by one reaction and pulled OFF by another.

19
00:01:19.603 --> 00:01:24.563
The OFF reaction sets how long each switch stays ON, and so how big the answer gets.

20
00:01:25.903 --> 00:01:30.503
And once the signal leaves, the response fades at about the pace of the slowest OFF

21
00:01:30.503 --> 00:01:31.503
step.

22
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:38.427
Now cholera. The toxin chemically modifies Gs alpha, so it can no longer cut its

23
00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:41.807
GTP. Gs is locked ON.

24
00:01:43.187 --> 00:01:47.727
Cyclic AMP never stops, and chloride and water pour into the intestine.

25
00:01:49.187 --> 00:01:51.507
Severe dehydration can kill within hours.

26
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.070
Another stuck switch.

27
00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.970
PKA sits clamped by regulatory subunits until cyclic AMP frees it.

28
00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.810
In some cases of Cushing's syndrome, its active subunit cannot bind the clamp.

29
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.730
Every OFF enzyme still works.

30
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.990
With no cyclic AMP, is PKA ON or OFF?

31
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:27.253
ON. PKA escapes its clamp, and cortisol production runs with no hormone telling it

32
00:02:27.253 --> 00:02:31.013
to. Nothing is wrong with the OFF enzymes here.

33
00:02:32.173 --> 00:02:34.353
The switch no longer needs its ON signal.

34
00:02:35.417 --> 00:02:40.377
So a switch sticks ON when it loses its OFF reaction, or stops needing its ON

35
00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:44.617
signal. The contest can also tip the other way.

36
00:02:45.797 --> 00:02:50.797
Weaken an ON arm, as in insulin resistance, and the response is too small.

37
00:02:51.817 --> 00:02:56.737
So the OFF reactions decide how big a cell's answer is, and how long it lasts.

38
00:02:57.977 --> 00:03:01.097
When one fails, the answer runs on without the signal.
